in the previous episode we realized that  a firebase project is actually a GCP  project under the hood and a data stored  in cloud storage can be equally accessed  via firebase powered mobile apps and web  clients as well as from your GCP hosted  applications in this episode we'll  explore two more products cloud  functions and cloud 5 store  stick around cloud functions is really a  google cloud product which also happens  to be visible from firebase cloud  functions is a popular implementation of  the functions as a service paradigm  where an event in a system such as an  HTTP call or a file uploaded to a bucket  triggers the execution of a small piece  of code a function check out the service  overview episode for more details on  cloud functions an important  architectural difference between the  firebase and GCP approach to cloud  functions is that firebase will offer  you to wrap your functions into callable  functions such functions can be invoked  via the firebase SDK with user tokens  and device instance IDs propagated  directly to the function another  important difference is the language is  supported when using firestore nodejs  and typescript are supported while go  Python and Java are also available when  using GCP call functions directly before  we compare web consoles you can also use  command-line interfaces g-cloud or the  firebase CLI both come with their own  set of functionalities and it's  recommended to decide which one works  best for you and stick to it  to deploy functions with firebase you'll  need to install firebase command-line  tools using NPM as deploying from the  firebase console is not possible in GCP  lion you can deploy functions straight  from the console integrating with GTP's  private source repositories or even  typing the function source code in line  firebase command-line tools do however  come with an API for strongly typed  handling of some trigger events and with  the ability to deploy multiple functions  at once just as you would expect a cloud  function created with a firebase project  can also be managed using cloud console  in fact clicking on detailed usage stats  in firebase will take you to that same  function only in cloud console where  you'll get additional features such as  monitoring graphs a tab to test the  function and the ability to set features  such as retry on failure memory  allocation or timeouts  whichever way you use cloth functions in  your firebase or GCP architecture  they're likely to be very effective at  what they do best small tasks in  reaction to all sorts of events cloud  fire store is Google's state-of-the-art  no SQL document database it is  schema-less and offers to store  documents containing attributes in a  hierarchy of collections it is fast it  supports transactions it's highly  available strongly consistent and it  does not require managing any kind of  infrastructure some will say it's a  service database cloud file store comes  in two flavors data store mode which is  mostly suitable for server projects and  native mode for mobile and web apps let  me focus here on the native mode which  is the one that is common to firebase  and GCP users this mode can  automatically scale to millions of  concurrent clients but what makes it  even more special is the fact that it  offers near real-time notifications this  is what enables synchronization of data  across devices with its built-in offline  support you can access and make changes  to your data and those changes will be  synced to the cloud when the client  comes back online data stored in cloud  fire store can be accessed using  firebase SDKs and of course you can use  either console to view and edit data and  indices and to monitor database access  usage with cloud fire store you can  query the database directly from your  mobile or web clients without the need  to setup an intermediary server to  manage access to your data and this is  why the firebase console has an  additional tab for security access rules  if you are a firebase user shipping an  app that accesses fire store then you  really should use firebase  authentication and think carefully about  you security access rules on the GCP  side there are no equivalent security  rules as you typically access via store  using a service account generally  speaking server-side code is considered  trusted as opposed to Klein code from  mobile apps  finally supported files store SDK  languages include Python node.js Java  c-sharp nod Go PHP and Ruby with  firebase mobile SDKs adding web  Android and iOS support these mobile  firebase SDKs include local caching as a  unique feature to help implement the  offline capability that I mentioned  earlier same project and billing  infrastructure common cloud storage  cloud functions and cloud fire store  services the two platforms have a lot in  common  so whether you're a GCP developer  interested in building mobile and web  apps with firebase or a firebase user  trying to grow your usage of GCP  services you should now have a good  sense of what's possible it's time to  get going with firebase or GCB or both  thanks for watching this video please  like subscribe comment share and look  forward to more GCP essentials videos  [Music]
 

